<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hotu Matuʻa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hotu Matua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hotu Matuʻa is the legendary founding chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> (Easter Island). Island tradition says he sailed from a homeland called Hiva, landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anakena">Anakena</a> Bay and divided the island among his sons, ancestors of its clans. His supposed burial place and the royal settlement at Anakena remained sacred to the island's ruling line.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the legend, the royal adviser Hau-Maka dreamed that his spirit travelled to a far country to look for new land for King Hotu Matuꞌa, and came to a place called Mata ki te rangi, Eyes that look to the sky. When he woke he told the king, who sent seven men from Hiva to find the island. They found it and returned, and the king then sailed there himself with his queen, Vakai. The colonising party travelled in two canoes, or in one double-hulled canoe, and the clans that divided the island claimed descent from his sons.</p><p>The island has also been called Te pito o te henua, the Center of the Earth. Hiva was the homeland of the islanders' legends; a comparison of the Rapanui language with other Polynesian languages suggests it was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a>. In all the early accounts the islanders insisted that the first inhabitants came from the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The details of the legend are unlikely to be verifiable. Linguistic, DNA and pollen evidence points to a first settlement by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a>, but its date is uncertain: published estimates range from about 300 to 400 CE to 700 to 800 CE, and a recent study, with radiocarbon dates from what is thought to be very early material, shows the island was settled by 1200 CE.</p><p>The likeness of his name to that of Atu Motua, Father Lord, an early founder god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangareva">Mangareva</a>, has led some historians to suspect that Hotu Matuꞌa entered the island's mythology only in the 1860s, when the Mangarevan language was also adopted. On that view the real founder was Tuꞌu ko Iho, who became a supporting figure in the legends centred on Hotu Matuꞌa.</p><p>The Norwegian ethnographer Thor Heyerdahl pointed to cultural likenesses with South American peoples and suggested that some settlers came from the continent. Mainstream archaeology doubts any non-Polynesian influence: an analysis of human remains from before European contact, made in 2017, found only Polynesian ancestry, while a 2020 study found contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> that likely took place in Eastern Polynesia before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> was settled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hotu Matuꞌa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Island tradition names the founding chief as <b>Hotu Matuʻa</b>, who sailed from a homeland called Hiva after seven scouts had found the island, landed at Anakena Bay on the north coast and divided the land among his sons, the ancestors of the island’s clans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
